feat(dashboard): draw the approved design across the app (RM-141)

The design was drawn, approved, and then two components of it were built.
This is the rest: the typography it specifies, the overview, the
verification page, the runs table -- and one real bug the artboards made
visible.

THE HEARTBEAT WAS RENDERED TWICE (FE-114). doc 06 section 3.1 puts the
anchoring pulse in the persistent header on every view, and the shell has
carried it there since RM-041. The overview body rendered a second
AnchoringPulse under it, reading the same endpoint through a second hook,
so a slow or failing second read put "anchored 3 min ago" directly above
"couldn't read the anchoring heartbeat" on one screen -- P2's collapse of
current and unknown, arrived at by accident. The body's copy is gone;
AnchoringEvidence stays, because chain positions, a segment digest and a
Rekor index are the material behind the claim and a one-line header cannot
carry them.

THREE FAMILIES, BUNDLED (FE-115, FE-116, FE-117). doc 06 section 5.2 was
amended by the operator on 2026-09-14 from two families to three: a display
serif for view headings and the largest metric figures, the sans for UI
text, the mono for every identifier. The sans and mono resolved to system
stacks and now resolve to IBM Plex, declared in web/src/app/fonts.css out of
packages in this build. Nothing is fetched: the faces ship in the image's
own asset directory, and the built stylesheet names no host.

The serif is restricted, and the restriction is a gate rather than a
convention. src/app/serif-discipline.test.ts holds a policy table naming
every style in the product entitled to the display face; spending it
somewhere new means adding a row where a reviewer reads it. Repo and
agent-type keep their mono view headings, because those headings are
identifiers and P4 outranks a handsome heading.

check-tokens.sh learned the amendment as part G: three families, none
fetched, each ending in a generic keyword -- the last because the
standalone page in web/site/ loads the sheet and bundles no faces. Three
new self-test cases prove the gate refuses each defect.

THE VIEWS. The overview's recent runs are a real table with status, run,
agent, task and commit columns (FE-118) instead of a stack of wrapped rows
that named none of its facts and that doc 06 section 6.4 does not allow.
That table and the runs table are now one treatment drawn from one module
(FE-121), compared by rendered class rather than by an import. The
verification page heads itself with the question it answers rather than
with the navigation item's words, and puts the SHA field and its control on
one row (FE-119). The three-check panel became a verdict band over three
side-by-side check cards with the attributed identity named under them
(FE-120) -- the fact a stranger came for, which was previously only
readable out of the mismatch comparison.

MEASURED, NOT ASSERTED. Moving the commit chip into the tinted verdict band
put text-ink-muted at 4.43:1 on the verified surface in dark mode, under
doc 06 section 6.4's floor; FE-100 caught it and the band's aside now
inherits its own ground's colour, which is the lesson tailwind-theme.css
already wrote down for the filled alert.

Twelve visual baselines regenerated in both platforms' Chromium: every
panel and alert-banner snapshot changes because the whole product's type
changed. web/site/public/tokens.css re-copied from the shipped sheet, which
had already drifted on main.

